WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
Traces and spans, precisely.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:10.243
OpenTelemetry (OTel), the vendor-neutral observability standard that most tools now speak, defines the vocabulary.

3
00:00:10.523 --> 00:00:16.517
A span is one unit of work: a model call, a tool call, a database query, a whole request.

4
00:00:16.797 --> 00:00:34.355
It has a name, a start and end time, attributes (key-value metadata such as the model id or token counts), optional events (timestamped notes, such as an exception), a status (Unset, Ok, or Error), and a kind (Client for an outgoing call, Server for an incoming one,

5
00:00:34.635 --> 00:00:38.795
Internal for in-process work, plus Producer and Consumer for queued work).

6
00:00:39.075 --> 00:00:41.976
A trace is all the spans that share one trace id.

7
00:00:42.256 --> 00:00:48.933
Each span also has its own span id and the id of its parent; the span with no parent is the root.

8
00:00:49.213 --> 00:01:00.072
Together, the trace id and span id form the span context, which is what gets passed along when one service calls another, so that the callee’s spans join the caller’s trace.

9
00:01:00.352 --> 00:01:05.067
Across HTTP this travels in the W3C traceparent header.

10
00:01:05.347 --> 00:01:07.629
Two consequences matter in practice.

11
00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:22.992
First, durations nest: a parent’s duration includes its children, so you find where time went by looking for the child with the largest share, and the parent’s self time (its duration minus its children) is time spent in your own code.

12
00:01:23.272 --> 00:01:25.576
Second, the trace id is a join key.

13
00:01:25.856 --> 00:01:34.965
Put it on every log record, every eval result, and every user-feedback event, and you can move from a bad thumbs-down to the exact trace that produced it.
